WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.120
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.090
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.050
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:14.800
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.320
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.320
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.019
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:23.840
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:26.469
it's part two of our three-part

00:00:26.469 --> 00:00:29.500
mini-series on famous con artists.

00:00:29.959 --> 00:00:32.860
In part one, we met Victor Lustig,

00:00:33.059 --> 00:00:35.439
the man who sold the Eiffel Tower.

00:00:35.789 --> 00:00:38.130
In part three, we'll meet Charles

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.199
Ponzi, the man whose scheme was

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.520
so spectacular that his name

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.649
became forever associated with it.

00:00:45.029 --> 00:00:47.855
But today, it's time to talk about Gregor

00:00:47.855 --> 00:00:51.379
MacGregor, the man who invented a country.

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:53.899
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.599
minute and get right into it.

00:00:59.029 --> 00:01:02.469
In the early 1820s, if you walked

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.440
through the City of London, you

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.189
could stop at a smart office and

00:01:07.199 --> 00:01:10.309
buy land in a country called Poyais.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.809
Poyais was a small, fertile paradise

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.589
on the coast of Central America.

00:01:17.519 --> 00:01:21.779
There was a 355-page guidebook describing

00:01:21.779 --> 00:01:24.999
it, full of maps and illustrations.

00:01:25.599 --> 00:01:28.589
There was a capital city called St Joseph,

00:01:28.929 --> 00:01:31.949
with wide paved boulevards, a theatre,

00:01:32.379 --> 00:01:35.510
an opera house, a cathedral, and a bank.

00:01:36.269 --> 00:01:38.259
You could buy Poyaisian government

00:01:38.269 --> 00:01:40.859
bonds on the London Stock Exchange.

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:43.299
You could exchange your British

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.459
pounds for Poyaisian dollars, crisp,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.482
beautifully printed banknotes.

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:51.249
If you were feeling adventurous,

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:52.950
you could even buy a ticket,

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.820
board a ship, and settle there.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:59.520
Around 250 men, women and

00:01:59.529 --> 00:02:02.029
children did exactly that.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:05.249
And when they arrived, after

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.279
two months at sea, they found no

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:11.009
boulevards, no opera house, no bank,

00:02:11.469 --> 00:02:14.369
no signs of civilisation whatsoever.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.930
They found an empty jungle.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.199
Poyais, after all, did not exist.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.019
It had never existed.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.710
Every map, every banknote, every bond

00:02:27.919 --> 00:02:30.469
and every page of that guidebook had

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.349
come from the imagination of one man: a

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.726
Scottish soldier named Gregor MacGregor.

00:02:39.039 --> 00:02:43.379
He was born on Christmas Eve, 1786, beside

00:02:43.389 --> 00:02:46.049
Loch Katrine in the Scottish Highlands.

00:02:47.229 --> 00:02:49.605
He was a member of Clan Gregor,

00:02:49.629 --> 00:02:52.059
the same clan as the famous outlaw

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.679
Rob Roy, a fact that he would make

00:02:54.679 --> 00:02:56.719
sure to point out to anyone he met.

00:02:58.199 --> 00:03:01.059
At sixteen, he joined the British Army.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.559
His family bought him the rank of junior

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:06.870
officer, which was a normal practice at

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:10.220
the time, and a couple of years later he

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.990
married into a well-off family, marrying

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.570
the daughter of a Royal Navy admiral.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.710
Her money bought him the rank of captain.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.789
And his fellow officers

00:03:22.949 --> 00:03:24.849
noticed two things about him.

00:03:25.539 --> 00:03:28.340
He was competent enough; not a bad

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.360
soldier, but not particularly remarkable.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.410
One thing that did seem strange was

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:37.369
that he was extraordinarily fond

00:03:37.369 --> 00:03:39.759
of uniforms, medals and titles.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.939
He loved them, and kept

00:03:41.969 --> 00:03:43.030
a small collection.

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:47.409
The army wasn't for him, though, and in

00:03:47.419 --> 00:03:51.310
1810, aged 23, he left, after getting

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:53.559
into a dispute with a senior officer.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.200
As a regular citizen back in Edinburgh,

00:03:57.539 --> 00:03:59.820
he continued to refer to himself by

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.679
his military title, but he decided

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.649
to give himself a little upgrade:

00:04:05.279 --> 00:04:08.090
"Colonel" MacGregor sounded good, so

00:04:08.379 --> 00:04:10.740
that was how he introduced himself.

00:04:11.389 --> 00:04:13.359
Colonel, by the way, is

00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:15.619
three levels up from captain.

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:18.599
Clearly, something about life in

00:04:18.599 --> 00:04:20.720
Edinburgh wasn't quite doing it for

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.169
him, so he headed to where the action

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.639
was really taking place: London.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:26.590
The capital.

00:04:27.599 --> 00:04:30.149
And when he arrived, he decided that

00:04:30.159 --> 00:04:33.079
he deserved yet another upgrade, and

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.449
started introducing himself to people

00:04:35.479 --> 00:04:38.620
as "Sir Gregor MacGregor, baronet".

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.109
He wasn't a Sir, he wasn't a baronet, he

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.185
was just regular old Gregor MacGregor,

00:04:44.427 --> 00:04:47.399
but this was England of the 19th

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.179
century and if people believed you

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:51.889
when you told them you were a member

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:54.079
of the Scottish aristocracy, well,

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.849
certain doors would open that remained

00:04:56.849 --> 00:04:59.359
closed to regular members of society.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.679
But shortly after arriving in London, his

00:05:03.689 --> 00:05:07.149
wife died, and with her went his income.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:10.909
It was time for yet another reinvention.

00:05:11.779 --> 00:05:14.879
MacGregor sold what he had and sailed

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.370
across the Atlantic, to Venezuela,

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:19.639
where a war of independence had

00:05:19.649 --> 00:05:21.639
just broken out against Spain.

00:05:22.989 --> 00:05:25.380
If London was a place where not

00:05:25.479 --> 00:05:27.399
too many questions were asked about

00:05:27.429 --> 00:05:30.169
a man's past, South America was

00:05:30.179 --> 00:05:32.119
on an entirely different level.

00:05:32.739 --> 00:05:34.899
Say it with confidence and a straight

00:05:34.899 --> 00:05:37.280
face, and have a convincing enough

00:05:37.339 --> 00:05:40.279
backstory, and people would believe you.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.510
He offered his services to the

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.709
revolutionary government, most likely

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.169
greatly exaggerating the extent of his

00:05:48.169 --> 00:05:50.050
military career in the British Army.

00:05:50.809 --> 00:05:53.789
But the rebels needed anyone they could.

00:05:54.641 --> 00:05:56.849
MacGregor joined up and rose

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.210
quickly through the ranks.

00:05:59.179 --> 00:06:00.659
He also married a local

00:06:00.659 --> 00:06:02.370
woman named Josefa.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.600
And she, in fact, did have genuine

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.770
prestigious family connections; she

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.770
was a cousin of Simón Bolívar himself,

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.539
the great liberator of South America.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.069
And as for MacGregor, he actually did

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:21.499
some real, half-decent soldiering.

00:06:22.479 --> 00:06:26.220
In 1816, he led a fighting retreat through

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:28.610
hundreds of miles of hostile territory,

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:31.939
pursued by a larger Spanish force for a

00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:35.199
month, and brought his army out intact.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.810
It was a real achievement, and

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.520
he was promoted to general.

00:06:40.309 --> 00:06:42.499
Now, there's probably an entire

00:06:42.579 --> 00:06:44.729
episode we could make simply on

00:06:44.729 --> 00:06:46.369
MacGregor's time in Latin America.

00:06:46.899 --> 00:06:48.499
He tried to declare his own

00:06:48.509 --> 00:06:50.420
republic on an island off the

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:52.140
coast of Florida, he spent time in

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.119
Jamaica, he went all over the place.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.119
It was a mixture of bold plans,

00:06:57.419 --> 00:07:00.229
unrealistic expectations, and running

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.409
for the hills when things went south.

00:07:02.989 --> 00:07:04.489
But we must get to the

00:07:04.489 --> 00:07:06.659
big trick, the main con.

00:07:08.029 --> 00:07:12.100
In 1820, MacGregor arrived on the Mosquito

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.360
Coast, which is a wild stretch on the east

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.089
of what is now Honduras and Nicaragua.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:21.870
It was ruled, loosely, by a local

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.579
king called George Frederic Augustus,

00:07:24.699 --> 00:07:26.370
who had good relations with Britain

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.280
and a fondness for European goods.

00:07:30.185 --> 00:07:33.389
MacGregor gave the king rum and

00:07:33.409 --> 00:07:36.129
jewellery, flattered him enthusiastically,

00:07:36.479 --> 00:07:38.980
and came away with a piece of paper

00:07:39.309 --> 00:07:41.550
granting him eight million acres of

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:44.830
land, a territory bigger than Wales.

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:47.389
The grant itself was real;

00:07:47.639 --> 00:07:49.169
the king really did sign it.

00:07:49.649 --> 00:07:52.929
But it was a grant of empty land,

00:07:53.259 --> 00:07:55.659
jungle, swamps and mangroves.

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:58.670
There were no towns, no roads, no

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:01.940
farms and barely anyone apart from a

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.309
few scattered indigenous communities.

00:08:05.689 --> 00:08:08.310
And importantly, it came with

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.929
no right to rule; the king had

00:08:10.929 --> 00:08:12.999
given him land, not a kingdom.

00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:16.300
That was not how MacGregor

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:17.429
chose to describe it.

00:08:18.479 --> 00:08:20.389
The following year, he returned

00:08:20.419 --> 00:08:22.340
to London as a new man.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:26.078
He was no longer a colonel or a baronet.

00:08:26.529 --> 00:08:29.630
He was now, he told people, "His Serene

00:08:29.639 --> 00:08:32.249
Highness Gregor, Cazique of Poyais".

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.619
A cazique, he explained,

00:08:34.879 --> 00:08:36.150
was a kind of prince.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:38.890
London society welcomed

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:40.509
him with open arms.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:44.119
He was, after all, a decorated general

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.039
of the South American wars, a clansman of

00:08:47.039 --> 00:08:50.069
Rob Roy, married into Bolívar's family.

00:08:50.649 --> 00:08:52.089
He dined with the Lord Mayor.

00:08:52.489 --> 00:08:54.499
His wife was received as

00:08:54.539 --> 00:08:55.889
the Princess of Poyais.

00:08:57.129 --> 00:08:59.940
He opened a Poyaisian legation in the City

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.240
of London, which was a sort of embassy,

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.020
and even began appointing officials.

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:07.970
He created a Poyaisian order of

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.020
chivalry, the Order of the Green

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:13.229
Cross, and pinned it on a few friends.

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.309
He even claimed to have attended

00:09:16.309 --> 00:09:18.099
the coronation of King George

00:09:18.109 --> 00:09:20.330
IV on behalf of his country.

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:24.330
Nobody seemed to have asked whether

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:25.910
anyone else had actually heard of

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.410
this country, the mythical-sounding

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:29.749
young nation of Poyais.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:31.950
It didn't seem to matter.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.350
This was a scam that

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.400
came at the perfect time.

00:09:37.649 --> 00:09:40.100
Britain in the early 1820s

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:42.079
was awash with money.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.969
The industrial revolution and the

00:09:44.969 --> 00:09:47.879
expanding empire had generated vast

00:09:47.879 --> 00:09:49.999
wealth for the country, and there was

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.780
a lot of cash sloshing around looking

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.099
for the next big investment opportunity.

00:09:55.879 --> 00:09:58.539
The Napoleonic Wars were over, and

00:09:58.549 --> 00:10:01.300
British government bonds paid very little.

00:10:02.389 --> 00:10:04.589
Meanwhile, the newly independent

00:10:04.589 --> 00:10:06.329
countries of South America were

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:08.659
all raising loans in London at

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:11.039
eye-wateringly high interest rates.

00:10:11.799 --> 00:10:13.550
Investors were hungry for

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:15.249
anything South American.

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:18.509
And it all seemed theoretical.

00:10:19.099 --> 00:10:20.829
Most Englishmen couldn't have

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:23.040
identified Colombia or Peru on a

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.949
map, so what did it matter if they

00:10:25.949 --> 00:10:27.310
hadn't heard of Poyais before?

00:10:28.049 --> 00:10:29.609
One more exotic-sounding name on

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:32.378
the list raised no eyebrows at all.

00:10:33.788 --> 00:10:36.069
But MacGregor invented

00:10:36.079 --> 00:10:37.659
much more than the name.

00:10:38.379 --> 00:10:39.769
He thought of everything.

00:10:40.769 --> 00:10:43.449
There was a 355-page guidebook

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:46.389
that came out in 1822 written by

00:10:46.389 --> 00:10:49.079
a "Captain Thomas Strangeways".

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.360
Nice name for an adventurous

00:10:52.399 --> 00:10:53.429
travel writer, you might

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:55.149
be thinking: Strangeways.

00:10:55.939 --> 00:10:57.949
There was no Captain Strangeways.

00:10:58.169 --> 00:11:01.079
No such man existed; the author was

00:11:01.079 --> 00:11:03.649
MacGregor and his small circle of helpers.

00:11:04.519 --> 00:11:06.959
Fake it might have been, but it

00:11:06.959 --> 00:11:09.099
was a wonderful piece of fantasy.

00:11:09.879 --> 00:11:11.469
The book described a land

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:13.489
of astonishing richness, and

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.039
described it in great detail.

00:11:16.989 --> 00:11:19.140
The soil of Poyais produced

00:11:19.149 --> 00:11:21.069
three harvests of maize a year.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.739
The forests were full of valuable

00:11:23.739 --> 00:11:26.059
timber, the waters full of fish,

00:11:26.139 --> 00:11:28.029
the land full of animals, to such

00:11:28.039 --> 00:11:30.480
an extent that "a man could hunt or

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.589
fish for a single day and bring back

00:11:32.629 --> 00:11:34.879
enough to feed his family for a week".

00:11:36.089 --> 00:11:38.319
The rivers, it claimed, carried

00:11:38.399 --> 00:11:40.699
pure gold, actual small lumps of

00:11:40.699 --> 00:11:42.739
it, washing down from the mountains.

00:11:43.419 --> 00:11:46.119
The capital, St Joseph, had twenty

00:11:46.119 --> 00:11:48.340
thousand inhabitants, public buildings,

00:11:48.569 --> 00:11:50.839
an opera house, and the Bank of Poyais.

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:54.450
The climate, the book insisted, was

00:11:54.509 --> 00:11:56.869
remarkably healthy for Europeans, a

00:11:56.869 --> 00:11:59.690
place where a colonist could relax and

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:01.420
recover from all manner of diseases.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:05.040
Most areas around it were terrible when it

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.520
comes to tropical disease but, according

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.920
to the book, Poyais was a veritable oasis.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.169
And on the back of all this,

00:12:14.344 --> 00:12:16.860
MacGregor began to sell.

00:12:17.879 --> 00:12:20.860
Land certificates went on sale at two

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:22.690
shillings and three pence per acre,

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.669
roughly a day's wages for a working man.

00:12:26.459 --> 00:12:29.029
The price rose steadily, to four

00:12:29.029 --> 00:12:31.099
shillings, and still people kept buying.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:33.499
Around five hundred people bought

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:35.689
Poyaisian land, with some people

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:38.779
putting in their entire life savings.

00:12:40.309 --> 00:12:43.058
Then, in October 1822,

00:12:43.419 --> 00:12:44.859
came the masterpiece.

00:12:46.059 --> 00:12:48.720
A respectable City bank underwrote a

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:52.369
loan of £200,000 for the government

00:12:52.379 --> 00:12:54.909
of Poyais, secured on the tax revenues

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:56.539
of that non-existent government,

00:12:57.139 --> 00:12:59.870
and the bonds went on sale on the

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:01.819
London Stock Exchange, sitting

00:13:01.819 --> 00:13:04.239
alongside the bonds of real countries.

00:13:05.659 --> 00:13:08.649
And to be fair to the investors, the

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:10.790
other "real" South American bonds

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:12.840
on the market were hardly safer.

00:13:13.739 --> 00:13:15.889
Within a few years, almost

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:17.619
every one of those new countries

00:13:17.949 --> 00:13:19.540
had stopped paying its debts.

00:13:20.059 --> 00:13:22.009
The whole market was a bubble, a

00:13:22.009 --> 00:13:25.239
period of wild, unrealistic investment.

00:13:26.029 --> 00:13:27.689
If nobody checks whether a

00:13:27.689 --> 00:13:29.689
country can pay, why does the

00:13:29.689 --> 00:13:31.720
country need to exist after all?

00:13:33.199 --> 00:13:35.610
And there was one more detail.

00:13:36.479 --> 00:13:38.700
MacGregor had banknotes printed in

00:13:38.709 --> 00:13:41.139
Edinburgh, by the same company that

00:13:41.139 --> 00:13:43.419
printed notes for the Bank of Scotland.

00:13:44.309 --> 00:13:46.859
When emigrants prepared to sail,

00:13:47.269 --> 00:13:49.569
his agents helpfully explained that

00:13:49.579 --> 00:13:51.299
British pounds would be of little

00:13:51.339 --> 00:13:54.229
use in Poyais, and exchanged the

00:13:54.239 --> 00:13:56.699
settlers' real money for MacGregor's

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:58.649
newly-minted Poyaisian dollars.

00:13:59.809 --> 00:14:01.919
Not only had these settlers bought

00:14:01.919 --> 00:14:04.859
land in a non-existent country, before

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:06.770
leaving they handed over the last

00:14:06.779 --> 00:14:09.239
real currency they had, their last

00:14:09.389 --> 00:14:12.259
actual money, for worthless banknotes.

00:14:13.119 --> 00:14:15.299
And these people, the people who

00:14:15.349 --> 00:14:17.879
fell for the ruse, were not fools.

00:14:18.599 --> 00:14:19.889
Mostly, they were

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:21.490
MacGregor's fellow Scots.

00:14:22.039 --> 00:14:24.469
Among them were doctors, lawyers,

00:14:24.739 --> 00:14:27.190
craftsmen and farmers, men with

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:29.609
families and respectable careers.

00:14:30.479 --> 00:14:32.659
And they went all in.

00:14:33.419 --> 00:14:35.709
Many of them sold their homes

00:14:35.709 --> 00:14:37.140
and their furniture to pay

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:38.280
for their piece of Poyais.

00:14:38.819 --> 00:14:41.269
They packed up their tools, said

00:14:41.269 --> 00:14:42.759
goodbye to friends and family,

00:14:42.899 --> 00:14:45.299
and sailed off into the sunset.

00:14:46.669 --> 00:14:48.219
MacGregor gave the more

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:50.159
ambitious ones titles and

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:52.289
government posts in the new land.

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:55.079
A shoemaker from Edinburgh proudly

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.550
accepted the position of Official

00:14:57.560 --> 00:14:59.670
Shoemaker to the Princess of Poyais.

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:04.819
In September 1822, the first

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.850
ship sailed from London with

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:08.659
about seventy settlers aboard.

00:15:09.599 --> 00:15:11.599
In January the following year, a

00:15:11.609 --> 00:15:13.660
second ship left from just outside

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:15.750
Edinburgh, with around two hundred more,

00:15:16.129 --> 00:15:18.660
and MacGregor proudly announced that

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.619
women and children would travel free.

00:15:21.779 --> 00:15:23.649
The ship left harbour with a

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:25.929
six-gun salute, the Poyaisian

00:15:25.959 --> 00:15:28.103
flag flying from the mast.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:32.029
All in all, around 250

00:15:32.029 --> 00:15:33.799
people sailed for Poyais.

00:15:34.739 --> 00:15:37.029
The crossing took around two months,

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:39.650
and you can imagine the mood on board.

00:15:40.349 --> 00:15:42.349
The settlers read and re-read

00:15:42.539 --> 00:15:43.900
their copies of the guidebook.

00:15:44.259 --> 00:15:46.219
They discussed which plots of land

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.489
they would farm, and which trades

00:15:48.619 --> 00:15:50.619
would be most in demand in the capital.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:52.539
The Official Shoemaker

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:54.199
checked over his tools.

00:15:55.359 --> 00:15:58.109
Somewhere below deck sat their furniture,

00:15:58.399 --> 00:16:00.608
their savings converted into Poyaisian

00:16:00.639 --> 00:16:02.810
dollars, and everything they would

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:04.589
need for the new life waiting for

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:06.289
them on the other side of the ocean.

00:16:07.769 --> 00:16:09.809
The first group reached the Mosquito

00:16:09.809 --> 00:16:13.539
Coast in November 1822, and found the

00:16:13.539 --> 00:16:15.600
mouth of the Black River, exactly where

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:17.959
the maps said St Joseph should be.

00:16:18.949 --> 00:16:21.449
But… there was nothing there.

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:24.049
No harbour, no city, no

00:16:24.059 --> 00:16:26.010
bank, no opera house.

00:16:26.519 --> 00:16:29.879
Just beach, swamp and jungle, and

00:16:30.059 --> 00:16:32.150
the crumbling remains of a few huts

00:16:32.329 --> 00:16:34.369
left by an earlier failed settlement.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:37.760
However, strangely enough, their

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:39.549
first thought doesn't seem to have

00:16:39.549 --> 00:16:41.779
been, "oh no, we've been tricked".

00:16:42.559 --> 00:16:43.409
Perhaps that thought

00:16:43.449 --> 00:16:45.289
was too big to let in.

00:16:46.229 --> 00:16:48.269
They decided that St Joseph must

00:16:48.269 --> 00:16:50.479
be further inland, or that the

00:16:50.479 --> 00:16:52.229
government of Poyais would shortly

00:16:52.229 --> 00:16:53.649
send officials to receive them.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:56.159
They unloaded their supplies,

00:16:56.529 --> 00:16:58.449
set up camp on the beach, and

00:16:58.459 --> 00:16:59.889
waited for someone to come.

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:02.359
The man MacGregor had put in charge

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:05.419
of the expedition, an ex-army officer

00:17:05.649 --> 00:17:07.879
named Hector Hall, grandly titled

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:09.959
Baron Tinto, kept sailing along

00:17:09.959 --> 00:17:13.380
the coast, supposedly to find help

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:15.429
or to consult the Mosquito king.

00:17:16.139 --> 00:17:17.899
Of course, he never found him.

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:20.680
The second ship arrived in

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:22.819
March, and two hundred more

00:17:22.819 --> 00:17:25.049
people walked into the same trap.

00:17:25.699 --> 00:17:28.269
Fresh faces came ashore asking

00:17:28.269 --> 00:17:30.619
for directions to St Joseph, and

00:17:30.889 --> 00:17:32.789
the first group had to tell them

00:17:33.103 --> 00:17:34.749
there were no directions to give.

00:17:35.839 --> 00:17:38.139
Then the rainy season came.

00:17:38.759 --> 00:17:40.209
The camp flooded.

00:17:40.699 --> 00:17:43.249
Malaria and yellow fever swept

00:17:43.259 --> 00:17:45.469
through the settlers, and they,

00:17:45.519 --> 00:17:47.580
remember, had been promised a climate

00:17:47.739 --> 00:17:49.879
remarkably healthy for Europeans.

00:17:51.129 --> 00:17:53.570
With no proper medicine, people

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:56.250
started to drop like flies.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:17:59.800
The man who had been appointed Official

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:01.820
Shoemaker to the non-existent Princess

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:05.220
of Poyais took a pistol and shot himself.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:08.569
Rescue, when it finally

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:10.860
came, was almost accidental.

00:18:12.039 --> 00:18:14.370
A small ship was passing by the coast

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:17.429
and saw this bunch of ragged Europeans.

00:18:18.029 --> 00:18:20.039
They took the survivors to British

00:18:20.039 --> 00:18:22.209
Honduras [which is now called Belize],

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:24.889
and it was there that they learned the

00:18:24.889 --> 00:18:28.009
truth: there was no Poyais, there never

00:18:28.009 --> 00:18:30.780
had been, and nobody in this part of the

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:33.450
world had ever heard of their Cazique.

00:18:34.626 --> 00:18:36.855
Word got back to the king who had

00:18:36.855 --> 00:18:39.435
granted MacGregor the land, and when

00:18:39.435 --> 00:18:40.805
he learned what the Scotsman had

00:18:40.805 --> 00:18:43.615
done, he publicly cancelled the grant.

00:18:45.085 --> 00:18:47.965
Of the roughly 250 settlers

00:18:48.145 --> 00:18:51.385
who sailed, at least 180 died.

00:18:52.025 --> 00:18:53.785
Fewer than fifty ever

00:18:53.785 --> 00:18:54.895
made it back to Britain.

00:18:56.445 --> 00:18:58.705
When the survivors' accounts reached

00:18:58.725 --> 00:19:01.715
the London newspapers in late 1823,

00:19:02.215 --> 00:19:04.435
there was an enormous scandal.

00:19:04.975 --> 00:19:07.625
But MacGregor had already slipped

00:19:07.645 --> 00:19:09.085
across the Channel to Paris.

00:19:10.125 --> 00:19:12.265
Strangely enough, though, some of

00:19:12.265 --> 00:19:14.475
the survivors of Poyais, some of

00:19:14.475 --> 00:19:16.415
the people who had been duped by

00:19:16.415 --> 00:19:18.915
MacGregor, publicly defended him.

00:19:20.105 --> 00:19:22.235
Six of them signed a public

00:19:22.235 --> 00:19:24.545
statement insisting that their

00:19:24.545 --> 00:19:26.815
Cazique was blameless, and that

00:19:26.825 --> 00:19:28.895
the disaster was the fault of the

00:19:28.905 --> 00:19:30.975
expedition's leaders on the ground.

00:19:32.315 --> 00:19:35.425
Perhaps this sounds impossible, but,

00:19:35.735 --> 00:19:38.005
just like with the victims of Victor

00:19:38.005 --> 00:19:40.565
Lustig, for some people it must have

00:19:40.565 --> 00:19:43.035
been too painful to admit that they had

00:19:43.035 --> 00:19:45.725
been tricked; it was easier to point to

00:19:45.725 --> 00:19:48.185
something else, to blame someone else.

00:19:49.335 --> 00:19:51.855
Now, if you've been having strange

00:19:51.865 --> 00:19:53.895
flashbacks, thinking "I'm sure I've

00:19:53.895 --> 00:19:55.905
heard a story of Scottish people

00:19:55.925 --> 00:19:57.655
trying to set up a colony in Central

00:19:57.655 --> 00:19:59.635
America before", you're right.

00:20:00.395 --> 00:20:02.165
A century and a quarter earlier,

00:20:02.505 --> 00:20:04.365
Scotland had tried to build its

00:20:04.385 --> 00:20:06.275
own colony in Central America,

00:20:06.595 --> 00:20:09.055
at Darién, in modern-day Panama.

00:20:09.945 --> 00:20:12.865
That too ended in disaster, and you

00:20:12.865 --> 00:20:15.751
can hear about that in episode 564.

00:20:16.755 --> 00:20:18.675
That, though, wasn't a con.

00:20:19.155 --> 00:20:20.885
A terrible idea, yes,

00:20:20.945 --> 00:20:22.825
but not one man's trick.

00:20:23.905 --> 00:20:25.685
Poyais was the invention

00:20:25.865 --> 00:20:27.669
of Gregor MacGregor.

00:20:28.495 --> 00:20:30.585
And even after being found

00:20:30.585 --> 00:20:32.395
out, the con continued.

00:20:33.095 --> 00:20:35.415
In Paris, MacGregor ran

00:20:35.425 --> 00:20:37.025
the same scheme again.

00:20:38.095 --> 00:20:39.855
He linked up with a French

00:20:39.885 --> 00:20:41.995
trading company, produced a new

00:20:42.045 --> 00:20:44.075
constitution for Poyais, and

00:20:44.345 --> 00:20:46.525
began recruiting French emigrants.

00:20:47.325 --> 00:20:49.005
This time, however, the

00:20:49.005 --> 00:20:50.195
authorities noticed.

00:20:50.805 --> 00:20:53.075
Officials became suspicious when

00:20:53.105 --> 00:20:55.055
a group of citizens applied for

00:20:55.055 --> 00:20:57.455
passports to travel to a country that

00:20:57.455 --> 00:20:59.605
didn't exist on any official map,

00:21:00.025 --> 00:21:02.285
and the whole network was arrested.

00:21:03.195 --> 00:21:05.595
In 1826, a French court

00:21:05.825 --> 00:21:07.685
tried MacGregor for fraud.

00:21:08.245 --> 00:21:10.125
But he had a plan.

00:21:11.045 --> 00:21:13.305
He hired excellent lawyers, presented

00:21:13.305 --> 00:21:15.355
himself as an honest soldier

00:21:15.395 --> 00:21:17.595
betrayed by dishonest associates,

00:21:17.855 --> 00:21:20.725
and was acquitted, found not guilty.

00:21:21.395 --> 00:21:23.045
One of his associates went

00:21:23.045 --> 00:21:25.475
to prison, but the mastermind

00:21:25.495 --> 00:21:27.585
of the operation walked free.

00:21:28.755 --> 00:21:30.785
He returned to London, and,

00:21:31.145 --> 00:21:32.865
incredibly, kept going.

00:21:33.495 --> 00:21:36.705
For almost a decade he continued to issue

00:21:36.735 --> 00:21:39.095
Poyaisian bonds and land certificates,

00:21:39.275 --> 00:21:41.645
in smaller and smaller schemes, to

00:21:41.645 --> 00:21:42.995
anyone who would still buy them.

00:21:44.555 --> 00:21:47.605
By 1838, his second wife had died,

00:21:47.945 --> 00:21:50.665
the money had dried up, and London

00:21:50.675 --> 00:21:52.485
had finally stopped listening.

00:21:53.205 --> 00:21:56.395
So the old soldier made his last move.

00:21:57.185 --> 00:21:59.915
He sailed to Venezuela, presented himself

00:21:59.915 --> 00:22:02.265
to the government as a hero of the war of

00:22:02.265 --> 00:22:05.305
independence, and asked for his old rank

00:22:05.305 --> 00:22:07.705
back, along with a general's pension.

00:22:08.555 --> 00:22:10.445
The Venezuelans said yes.

00:22:11.585 --> 00:22:14.135
Gregor MacGregor spent his final years in

00:22:14.135 --> 00:22:17.725
Caracas as a respected veteran, and when

00:22:17.725 --> 00:22:21.645
he died in 1845, he was buried with full

00:22:21.665 --> 00:22:23.965
military honours in Caracas Cathedral.

00:22:25.175 --> 00:22:27.745
He, of the three protagonists in

00:22:27.745 --> 00:22:29.845
this mini-series, is the only one

00:22:29.845 --> 00:22:31.695
to have truly "got away with it".

00:22:32.485 --> 00:22:34.185
And given the scale of the

00:22:34.185 --> 00:22:36.225
con, it is quite something.

00:22:36.695 --> 00:22:39.325
An entirely new country, invented.

00:22:39.695 --> 00:22:40.255
Guidebooks.

00:22:40.435 --> 00:22:40.965
Currency.

00:22:41.305 --> 00:22:42.665
Even persuading London

00:22:42.675 --> 00:22:44.555
banks to issue debt for it.

00:22:45.305 --> 00:22:47.505
On the list of the most ambitious con

00:22:47.535 --> 00:22:50.375
artists of all time, it's hard to find

00:22:50.375 --> 00:22:52.745
a more worthy number one than Gregor

00:22:52.745 --> 00:22:55.438
MacGregor, the Cazique of Poyais.

00:22:56.815 --> 00:22:59.035
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:22:59.035 --> 00:23:00.372
episode on Gregor MacGregor,

00:23:00.605 --> 00:23:02.285
the man who invented a country.

00:23:02.595 --> 00:23:03.875
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:23:04.155 --> 00:23:05.755
and that you've learnt something new.

00:23:06.255 --> 00:23:08.635
Next up, in the final part of this

00:23:08.635 --> 00:23:10.975
mini-series, we'll be off to Boston

00:23:11.155 --> 00:23:13.555
in the summer of 1920, where an

00:23:13.555 --> 00:23:15.865
Italian immigrant named Charles Ponzi

00:23:16.135 --> 00:23:18.315
discovered a magical way to double

00:23:18.365 --> 00:23:20.555
people's money, and got immortalised

00:23:20.585 --> 00:23:22.455
in the dictionary in the process.

00:23:23.105 --> 00:23:25.035
You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:25.035 --> 00:23:27.775
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:23:28.145 --> 00:23:31.245
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:23:31.285 --> 00:23:33.405
I'll catch you in the next episode.

